[#4471](/kb/incidents/4471) says one issue, one fix queued. The reports in it say three.
This page is built from the same export the desk reads, so the numbers below move when the
desk merges more.

<Callout kind="warn">
Three problems share this ticket. Each one needs its own ticket, because one fix closes one of
them and leaves the other two open.
</Callout>

## What the reports hold

<ProblemTable ticket={4471} {tickets} />

## The three problems

**`parser`: the shared parser reads every date month first.** Our tenants write the day
first, so 7 April becomes 4 July. This is the problem #4471 describes, and it is most of the
ticket. It reaches people through the app, by email and through the booking-v2 form.

**`hint`: the booking-v3 form prints `MM/DD/YYYY` under its date field and then reads the
field the tenant's way.** People type what the hint tells them, month first, and the form flips
it back. Only the three tenants on booking-v3 see it, and only through the form. The parser fix
does not touch this: the form has already stored the wrong day before the parser runs.

**`one-day-move`: the invite lands one day off, not one month off.** Lisbon gets Tuesday and
Toronto gets Monday for the same meeting; one invite arrived before its booking was made. That is
a time zone or day boundary, not a day/month swap. The desk already read these five reports as a
one-day move (`desk-notes.json`) but filed them under `parser` by tenant. Here they count once,
under their own problem.

## How the counts are made

- One id, one report: the export pages overlap, so `uniqueById` drops the repeated rows first.
- A report's problem comes from its tenant and its way in (`causes.ts`), not from the intake
  tag. Intake guesses from the reporter's browser locale and is wrong on the reports whose
  browser locale differs from the tenant's locale.
- A report the desk read as a one-day move (`desk-notes.json`) is its own problem and is not
  counted under `parser` again.
- Every report sits in exactly one problem, so the three counts add up to the merged total.

## Why the desk missed it

The [dedupe rule](/kb/triage/dedupe) merges on subject line alone. Twelve subject lines cover all
three problems, and none of them names a problem, so every report matched an open ticket and
nobody read a body. [Decision 0001](/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject) accepts that reports with
different subjects split; it did not expect reports with the same subject to hide different bugs.

## What to do

1. Keep #4471 for `parser` only, with its count from the table above.
2. Open one ticket each for `hint` and `one-day-move` (filed as the tickets linked in the table).
3. When a fix ships, close its ticket and re-run this page: a count that does not fall to zero
   means the fix missed.

